    {
      "id": "ituYJj5hdFK0VHwybOCfi",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1279.8830180812292,
      "y": 507.17519533451195,
      "width": 942.7627873861902,
      "height": 1485.1155063206982,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#eebefa",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b07",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 82440605,
      "version": 1629,
      "versionNonce": 773924733,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781515862229,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) How Can We Improve Pagination Consistency and Efficiency?\n- The problem: Traditional pagination uses \"give me page 2, page 3...\" with an OFFSET. But in a\nlive news feed, new articles arrive constantly. If you're on \"page 2\" and 5 new articles arrive,\neverything shifts down you might see the same article twice or skip some. Annoying!\n- The solution: Keyset Pagination (also called cursor-based pagination).\nInstead of asking for \"page 2,\" the app says: \"Give me the next 20 articles that come after the\nlast one I saw.\" It uses the last article's timestamp or ID as a bookmark (a \"cursor\").\n    > No duplicates, no skips, even as new articles pour in.\n    > Much faster, because the database can jump straight to the bookmark instead of counting\nthrough thousands of rows.\n\n2) How Do We Achieve Low Latency (< 200ms) Feed Requests?\n- Pre-compute feeds: Instead of building the feed when the user asks, we build it ahead of time\nand store it in a fast in-memory cache like Redis.\n- CDN (Content Delivery Network): Store popular content on servers physically close to users\naround the world, so data travels a shorter distance.\n\n3) How Do We Ensure Articles Appear Within 30 Minutes of Publication?\n- Smart polling frequency: Poll high-volume publishers (like major news sites) very often (every\nminute or two); poll smaller blogs less often.\n- Streaming pipeline: As soon as a poller finds a new article, it flows immediately through Kafka →\nparser → database, rather than waiting for a scheduled batch job.\n- Prioritization: Breaking-news sources get checked most frequently.\n\n4) How Do We Handle Media Content (Images/Videos) Efficiently?\n- Key principle: Don't store the actual heavy files — store the links and let a CDN do the heavy\nlifting.\n- We store only the URL of an image/video, plus maybe a small thumbnail.\n- Heavy media is served through a CDN so it loads fast worldwide.\n- Lazy loading: Images load only when they're about to scroll into view, saving bandwidth.\n\n5) How Do We Handle Traffic Spikes During Breaking News?\n- Auto-scaling: Automatically add more servers when traffic surges, then remove them when it\ncalms down.\n- Aggressive caching: During breaking news, everyone wants the same top stories — so one cached\ncopy serves millions.\n- Load balancers: Spread incoming requests evenly across many servers so none get overwhelmed.\n- Rate limiting: Gently cap abusive or excessive requests to protect the system.\n\n------Bonus Deep Dives-------\n\n6) How Can We Support Category-Based Feeds (Sports, Politics, Tech)?\n- Tag every article with one or more categories during the parsing step. This can be done using\nkeyword rules or a machine-learning classifier (a program that learns to sort articles into topics).\nStore the category as a field in the database.\n- The API then accepts a filter, e.g., GET /api/v1/feed?category=sports, returning only matching\narticles.\n- We can pre-build a cached feed per category for speed.\n\n7) How Do We Generate Personalized Feeds?\n- This tailors the feed to each user's interests based on their behavior.\n- Track signals: Which articles a user clicks, how long they read, what topics they favor.\n- Build a profile: \"This user loves tech and sports, ignores politics.\"\n- Rank accordingly: A ranking algorithm scores candidate articles higher if they match the user's\ninterests.\n- Two common approaches:\n    > Content-based filtering → \"You read tech articles, so here's more tech.\"\n    > Collaborative filtering → \"People similar to you liked these, so you might too.\"\n- Personalized feeds are usually pre-computed in the background and cached, keeping them fast to\nserve.\n",
      "fontSize": 19.47692467305834,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) How Can We Improve Pagination Consistency and Efficiency?\n- The problem: Traditional pagination uses \"give me page 2, page 3...\" with an OFFSET. But in a live news feed, new articles arrive constantly. If you're on \"page 2\" and 5 new articles arrive, everything shifts down you might see the same article twice or skip some. Annoying!\n- The solution: Keyset Pagination (also called cursor-based pagination).\nInstead of asking for \"page 2,\" the app says: \"Give me the next 20 articles that come after the last one I saw.\" It uses the last article's timestamp or ID as a bookmark (a \"cursor\").\n    > No duplicates, no skips, even as new articles pour in.\n    > Much faster, because the database can jump straight to the bookmark instead of counting through thousands of rows.\n\n2) How Do We Achieve Low Latency (< 200ms) Feed Requests?\n- Pre-compute feeds: Instead of building the feed when the user asks, we build it ahead of time and store it in a fast in-memory cache like Redis.\n- CDN (Content Delivery Network): Store popular content on servers physically close to users around the world, so data travels a shorter distance.\n\n3) How Do We Ensure Articles Appear Within 30 Minutes of Publication?\n- Smart polling frequency: Poll high-volume publishers (like major news sites) very often (every minute or two); poll smaller blogs less often.\n- Streaming pipeline: As soon as a poller finds a new article, it flows immediately through Kafka → parser → database, rather than waiting for a scheduled batch job.\n- Prioritization: Breaking-news sources get checked most frequently.\n\n4) How Do We Handle Media Content (Images/Videos) Efficiently?\n- Key principle: Don't store the actual heavy files — store the links and let a CDN do the heavy lifting.\n- We store only the URL of an image/video, plus maybe a small thumbnail.\n- Heavy media is served through a CDN so it loads fast worldwide.\n- Lazy loading: Images load only when they're about to scroll into view, saving bandwidth.\n\n5) How Do We Handle Traffic Spikes During Breaking News?\n- Auto-scaling: Automatically add more servers when traffic surges, then remove them when it calms down.\n- Aggressive caching: During breaking news, everyone wants the same top stories — so one cached copy serves millions.\n- Load balancers: Spread incoming requests evenly across many servers so none get overwhelmed.\n- Rate limiting: Gently cap abusive or excessive requests to protect the system.\n\n------Bonus Deep Dives-------\n\n6) How Can We Support Category-Based Feeds (Sports, Politics, Tech)?\n- Tag every article with one or more categories during the parsing step. This can be done using keyword rules or a machine-learning classifier (a program that learns to sort articles into topics).\nStore the category as a field in the database.\n- The API then accepts a filter, e.g., GET /api/v1/feed?category=sports, returning only matching articles.\n- We can pre-build a cached feed per category for speed.\n\n7) How Do We Generate Personalized Feeds?\n- This tailors the feed to each user's interests based on their behavior.\n- Track signals: Which articles a user clicks, how long they read, what topics they favor.\n- Build a profile: \"This user loves tech and sports, ignores politics.\"\n- Rank accordingly: A ranking algorithm scores candidate articles higher if they match the user's interests.\n- Two common approaches:\n    > Content-based filtering → \"You read tech articles, so here's more tech.\"\n    > Collaborative filtering → \"People similar to you liked these, so you might too.\"\n- Personalized feeds are usually pre-computed in the background and cached, keeping them fast to serve.\n",
