    {
      "id": "HSqc5H8eZJNjarL9VIP-z",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 302.593337496893,
      "y": 220.51510137050332,
      "width": 916.2693684895834,
      "height": 1375,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0I",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1106172592,
      "version": 10381,
      "versionNonce": 148083091,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781516866144,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "\n1) Functional Requirements:  \n- Aggregating a feed of news articles from thousands of global publishers.\n- Enabling users to \"infinitely\" scroll through the news feed.\n- Allowing users to click on an article and be redirected to the original publisher's website.\n\n2) Scale Understanding \n- User Base: 100M DAU \n- Content Volume: 10M new Articles / day \n- Read-to-Write Ratio: 100:1 \n\n2) Non Functional Requirements (SPARCS):  \n- Availability over Consistency: The system should almost never go down. If a user sees a\nnews article that's a few minutes old (slightly \"stale\"), that's totally fine. Nobody is harmed\nif a headline shows up 2 minutes late. So we prefer \"always on\" over \"always perfectly up-to-\ndate.\"\n- Low Latency: When a user opens the app, the feed should load in under 200 milliseconds\n(that's faster than a blink). New articles should appear in feeds within 30 minutes of being\npublished.\n\n3) BOE Calculations \n- Storage (how much disk space)\n    > Say each article (text + metadata like title, author, URL) is about 50 KB.\n    > 10 million articles/day × 50 KB = 500 GB per day.\n    > Over a year: 500 GB × 365 ≈ 180 TB per year.\n- Bandwidth (network traffic)\n    > Incoming (pulling articles from publishers): 500 GB/day ÷ 86,400 seconds ≈ 6\nMB/second.\n    > Outgoing (sending feeds to users) is much larger because of the read-heavy nature —\npotentially gigabytes per second at peak.\n- Request Load (QPS = Queries Per Second)\n    > 100 million users, each refreshing their feed ~10 times a day = 1 billion reads/day.\n    > 1 billion ÷ 86,400 seconds ≈ ~11,500 QPS on average, with peaks several times higher.\n\n4) System Interface\nThink of the system as a box. What goes in, and what comes out. \n- Inputs (news content coming in):\n    > RSS/Atom feeds (explained in detail below — this is the main one!)\n    > News APIs (some publishers offer direct data feeds)\n    > Web crawlers (programs that visit websites and read their content)\n- Outputs (the feed going out):\n    > A clean, ranked news feed delivered to the user's phone or web browser through an API.\n\n5) Data Flow\n- Data Ingestion flow \n    > Pollers/Crawlers regularly check publisher RSS feeds for new articles.\n    > New articles are dropped into a message queue (like Kafka) — a waiting line that keeps\nthings organized.\n    > Parsers clean up the data (extract title, text, image, source URL).\n    > Cleaned articles are saved into the database.\n- Feed Generation Flow \n    > User opens the app → request hits the API Gateway.\n    > The feed service fetches and ranks articles from the database.\n    > The ranked feed is returned to the user — fast.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\n1) Functional Requirements:  \n- Aggregating a feed of news articles from thousands of global publishers.\n- Enabling users to \"infinitely\" scroll through the news feed.\n- Allowing users to click on an article and be redirected to the original publisher's website.\n\n2) Scale Understanding \n- User Base: 100M DAU \n- Content Volume: 10M new Articles / day \n- Read-to-Write Ratio: 100:1 \n\n2) Non Functional Requirements (SPARCS):  \n- Availability over Consistency: The system should almost never go down. If a user sees a news article that's a few minutes old (slightly \"stale\"), that's totally fine. Nobody is harmed if a headline shows up 2 minutes late. So we prefer \"always on\" over \"always perfectly up-to-date.\"\n- Low Latency: When a user opens the app, the feed should load in under 200 milliseconds (that's faster than a blink). New articles should appear in feeds within 30 minutes of being published.\n\n3) BOE Calculations \n- Storage (how much disk space)\n    > Say each article (text + metadata like title, author, URL) is about 50 KB.\n    > 10 million articles/day × 50 KB = 500 GB per day.\n    > Over a year: 500 GB × 365 ≈ 180 TB per year.\n- Bandwidth (network traffic)\n    > Incoming (pulling articles from publishers): 500 GB/day ÷ 86,400 seconds ≈ 6 MB/second.\n    > Outgoing (sending feeds to users) is much larger because of the read-heavy nature — potentially gigabytes per second at peak.\n- Request Load (QPS = Queries Per Second)\n    > 100 million users, each refreshing their feed ~10 times a day = 1 billion reads/day.\n    > 1 billion ÷ 86,400 seconds ≈ ~11,500 QPS on average, with peaks several times higher.\n\n4) System Interface\nThink of the system as a box. What goes in, and what comes out. \n- Inputs (news content coming in):\n    > RSS/Atom feeds (explained in detail below — this is the main one!)\n    > News APIs (some publishers offer direct data feeds)\n    > Web crawlers (programs that visit websites and read their content)\n- Outputs (the feed going out):\n    > A clean, ranked news feed delivered to the user's phone or web browser through an API.\n\n5) Data Flow\n- Data Ingestion flow \n    > Pollers/Crawlers regularly check publisher RSS feeds for new articles.\n    > New articles are dropped into a message queue (like Kafka) — a waiting line that keeps things organized.\n    > Parsers clean up the data (extract title, text, image, source URL).\n    > Cleaned articles are saved into the database.\n- Feed Generation Flow \n    > User opens the app → request hits the API Gateway.\n    > The feed service fetches and ranks articles from the database.\n    > The ranked feed is returned to the user — fast.\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
